<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</h1><p class="st-meta">1987 CE – 1991 CE · capital Addis Ababa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The civilian-styled communist state that succeeded the Derg in 1987, led by Mengistu Haile Mariam until rebels took Addis Ababa in 1991.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A new constitution, approved by referendum in February 1987, turned the military government of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/derg">Derg</a> into the People's Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in September 1987. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu Haile Mariam</a> became president, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/workers-party-of-ethiopia">Workers' Party of Ethiopia</a> was proclaimed the leading force of state and society.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The PDRE was a one-party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxist-Leninist</a> state on the Soviet model, with collective farms, forced villagisation and resettlement of northerners to the south and west. Its army, among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa's</a> largest, was badly overstretched by wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a> and the Tigrayan-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a>, and suffered a heavy defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-afabet">Afabet</a> in 1988. Soviet aid dwindled as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> ended.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rebels captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> in 1990 and advanced on the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> on 21 May 1991, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on 28 May, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transitional-government-of-ethiopia">Transitional Government of Ethiopia</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">EPLF</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia_(1987%E2%80%931991).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1987 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Addis Ababa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mengistu Haile Mariam</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amharic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>PDRE</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1989&amp;focus=peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia" aria-label="People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5750 1840 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6070 2096L6070 2096M6070 2096L6070 2096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6070 2096h0M6070 2096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 2096h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 2096h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Addis Ababa. <a href="/atlas?year=1989&amp;focus=peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
